Instruction: Read the following excerpt from a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the carveout treatment. 
Question: Is the “ability to consummate” concept subject to Material Adverse Effect carveouts?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement Excerpt:
“Acquisition MAE” means any change, event, condition or circumstance, whether occurring individually or in aggregate, that either (i) has, or would reasonably be expected to have, a material adverse effect on the operational, financial or business conditions of the Target and its Subsidiaries, or (ii) would materially delay, impair or otherwise hinder the Target’s ability to consummate the Transaction by the Closing Date. 
Provided, however, that for purposes of determining a material adverse effect under clause (i), none of the following shall be considered: 
   (A) General economic downturns, including shifts in global economic conditions, fluctuations in currency exchange rates or interest rate adjustments;
   (B) Industry-wide challenges or common market movements affecting similarly situated entities;
   (C) Regulatory or GAAP accounting changes implemented after the signing of this Agreement;
   (D) Public announcements or disclosures relating to the Agreement;
   (E) Performance results that fall minorly short of internal budgets or published forecasts.
Notwithstanding the foregoing, with respect to clause (ii), no exception or carve-out shall apply. That is, any event or circumstance affecting the Target’s ability to consummate the Transaction shall be assessed in full without applying any of the exceptions listed under clause (i).

